  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Investing in the future of critical wildlife waters of northern Sonora - tools for cross-border.springs conservation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Springs are keystone ecosystems that have enormous effects on surrounding landscapes, biota, and economies and play a crucial role in providing refugia and critical sources of water for migratory birds, reptiles and amphibians, and wide-ranging wildlife. Springs&apos; ecosystems have experienced a history of human use, overuse and mismanagement, such as water diversion, invasive species, overgrazing, pollution, and groundwater extraction and/or contamination. The long term goal of this project is to inventory and assess the information available for 50% of springs in Sonora so it is consistent across the Sky Islands bi-national region so informed conservation, protection and restoration strategies can be made. Through this grant, Sky Islands Alliance will: 1) increase capacity for conservation of spring ecosystems in the Sonoran Sky Island Region by training at least 25 Sonoran agency personnel, land owners, university students, and volunteers in springs inventory, assessment, and data management protocols; 2) enhance cross-border spring data collaboration and sharing by creating bilingual resources and an online support networking for land managers; and 3) educate private landowners about the ecological values of springs and best management options. Project products include: a field datasheet translated into Spanish, springs assessment instructions translated into Spanish, a data entry portal website in Spanish, and reports for each spring assessed as generated by the database.</narrative>
  </description>
